WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.640
Hey, it's Laura, welcome to my blog post.

00:03.640 --> 00:07.340
I wrote this because most of the RFCs that I've been seeing,

00:07.340 --> 00:11.280
they actually hide the decision, and then nobody reads them.

00:11.280 --> 00:15.690
You've probably opened one, if not a ton of them,

00:15.690 --> 00:21.000
and it's like almost three pages, and you still don't know what you're being asked for,

00:21.000 --> 00:25.320
or what exact goal they're trying to get out of the RFC.

00:25.320 --> 00:31.420
I remember that a manager at a previous company once pointed me to a habit from the military.

00:31.420 --> 00:35.130
It's called BLUF, I think that's the way it's pronounced,

00:35.130 --> 00:37.960
and it means bottom line up front.

00:37.960 --> 00:40.960
Anyway, this post is everything that I've learned,

00:40.960 --> 00:44.000
and that I'm still learning, while writing RFCs.

00:44.000 --> 00:48.320
Hopefully it helps you on a daily basis, and it helps your team as well.

00:48.320 --> 00:51.040
Keep scrolling, and I'll be here in the corner.
